.TH MU2EDAQ-POWER-RECOVERY.YAML 5 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
power-recovery.yaml \- main configuration for the Mu2e DAQ power-recovery tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I config/power-recovery.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
A YAML mapping of sections, each holding scalar settings. Every key can be
overridden without editing this file.
.SS Precedence
Lowest priority first:
.IP 1. 4
built-in defaults, so the tools still run with
.I config/
missing entirely;
.IP 2. 4
this file;
.IP 3. 4
.IR config/.env ;
.IP 4. 4
the process environment;
.IP 5. 4
the command line.
.SS Environment names
The environment-variable spelling of any key is mechanical:
.BI MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ PATH
with the dotted path upper-cased and dots replaced by underscores, so
.I report.publish.target
is
.BR MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_REPORT_PUBLISH_TARGET .
A key added to this file is immediately overridable from the environment with
no code change. A variable naming a key no section below defines is applied
anyway, as a string: an operator running an older checkout against a newer
.I .env
is told nothing is wrong, rather than being stranded.
.PP
.B VAULT_ADDR
is also honoured, overriding
.IR vault.addr ,
because it is the name the
.BR vault (1)
CLI and
.I hvac
already use.
.B VAULT_TOKEN
is read directly by the Vault client (see
.I vault.auto_login
below) and is not a settings key.
.SS The .env file
One
.IR KEY = VALUE
per line. Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored, a leading
.B export
is stripped, and a value wrapped in matching single or double quotes is
unquoted. No interpolation is performed, deliberately: a password containing
.B $
must survive the round trip intact. Only
.BI MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ PATH
names are consumed from it.
.SS Value types
A value arriving from
.I .env
or the environment is a string, and is coerced to the type the key already
holds:
.IP \(bu 3
boolean:
.BR 1 ", " true ", " yes ", " on
and
.BR 0 ", " false ", " no ", " off ,
case-insensitively; anything else is an error naming the key;
.IP \(bu 3
integer and floating point: as written, or an error;
.IP \(bu 3
list: comma-separated, with surrounding whitespace stripped, so
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_TOPOLOGY_LOCATIONS=mc2,mc1
is a two-element list and an empty string is an empty list;
.IP \(bu 3
a key whose current value is
.B null
has no type to coerce to, so the string is passed through unchanged.
.PP
A list written in this file
.I replaces
the built-in default rather than extending it. That applies to every list key,
not just
.IR ssh.options :
an operator who writes a list means "these", not "these as well as whatever
was already there".
.SS What is recorded
The whole effective configuration \(em after every layer has been applied \(em
is written into the run record and into the report, so a run can be explained
months later without guessing which file was in place. Before it is written,
any value whose key ends in
.BR pass ", " passwd ", " password ", " token ", " secret " or " key
is replaced by
.BR <redacted> ,
rather than dropped, so the report still shows that a credential was
configured. Nothing else is filtered: a secret put under a key not matching
that pattern will appear in the report and in the logbook entry.
.SH SECTIONS
.SS run
.TP
.I label
Label for the recovery, shown in the report and the logbook subject.
.TP
.I dry_run
Default
.BR true .
While true, no power command is issued.
.B \-\-execute
sets it false \(em but it is
.I not
a second, independent gate. This is an ordinary configuration key, so setting it
false here, in
.IR config/.env ,
or as
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_RUN_DRY_RUN=false
arms the destructive path on its own, with no flag on the command line. Nothing
re-checks that
.B \-\-execute
was given. The run prints
.B "LIVE RUN \-\- power commands WILL be issued."
when it is armed.
.IP
.B \-\-simulate
is the gate no configuration file can open: it forces this key back to true
whatever else was asked for, including
.B \-\-execute
on the same command line.
.TP
.I stop_on_stage_failure
Default
.BR true .
Stop phase 2 at the first stage that does not meet its requirement.
.TP
.I phase_timeout
Intended wall-clock budget for one phase, in seconds; 0 disables.
.B Not currently enforced:
the value is read from the file and carried in the run record, but no phase
consults it. The effective bounds on a run are the per-command ones,
.I ssh.command_timeout
and
.IR ipmi.timeout .
.TP
.IR from_stage ", " until_stage
Default stage range for phase 2; normally set with
.BR \-\-from / \-\-until .
.SS topology
.TP
.IR file ", " sequence_file ", " checks_file
Names of the other configuration files, resolved inside
.IR config/ .
.TP
.I locations
Locations included when
.B \-\-location
is not given. Default:
.BR "[mc2, teststand]" .
.SS selfupdate
.TP
.I enabled
Run the phase-0 update check. Default
.BR true .
.TP
.IR remote ", " branch
Git remote and branch to compare against.
.I branch
null means the checked-out branch.
.TP
.I rebuild_globs
Paths whose modification by a pull triggers a rebuild.
.TP
.I allow_dirty
Default
.BR false .
When false, a working tree with local modifications is reported and left alone
rather than stashed.
.TP
.I timeout
Seconds allowed for the git round trip before giving up and running the local
revision.
.SS ssh
.TP
.IR user ", " root_user
Logins for ordinary and root sessions.
.I user
null means no
.IB login @
prefix is put on the ssh command line at all, leaving the choice to
.BR ssh_config (5)
and then to ssh's own default of the local username. On the DAQ hosts that is
what supplies
.BR mu2edaq ,
and it is why this is left null.
.IP
The login is deliberately
.I not
derived from the Kerberos principal. That was tried and reverted: with a valid
personal ticket, logging in as
.B mu2edaq
or
.B root
succeeds because those accounts'
.I .k5login
files authorise the personal principal, while an account named after the
principal does not exist on those hosts. Set this only to pin a login that
.BR ssh_config (5)
gets wrong.
.TP
.I proxy
Jump host.
.B auto
picks the first responsive gateway of the node's location;
.B none
disables proxying; anything else is used verbatim.
.TP
.IR connect_timeout ", " command_timeout
Seconds.
.TP
.I options
Options passed to every
.BR ssh (1)
invocation. A list here
.I replaces
the defaults rather than extending them.
.B BatchMode=yes
is important: it turns a missing ticket into a fast, legible failure instead of
a worker thread blocked on a prompt nobody can see.
.TP
.I max_sessions
Maximum simultaneous SSH sessions across the run.
.SS ipmi
.TP
.I execute_on
Records where
.BR ipmitool (1)
runs.
.B Not currently consulted:
ipmitool is always run on a gateway, because the IPMI subnets
(192.168.157.0/24, 192.168.150.0/24) are not routable from off site. Changing
this value has no effect; it is here so the intent is visible in the file and
in the run record.
.TP
.I username
BMC username. Null takes it from the Vault secret's
.I username
field. Set it here when the two disagree: IPMI usernames are case sensitive,
the working upstream invocation hard-codes
.BR MU2E ,
and the Vault secret has been seen holding
.BR mu2e .
.BR "mu2e-ipmi-tool \-\-diagnose"
finds the combination that works.
.TP
.IR tool ", " interface ", " privilege ", " cipher_suite
Passed to
.BR ipmitool .
Some BMCs need
.I cipher_suite: 17
rather than 3.
.TP
.IR timeout ", " retries
Per-command budget in seconds, and how many times this tool re-runs the whole
invocation after a failure (so
.I retries: 2
means up to three attempts). The gateway-side
.BR timeout (1)
wrapper is set to
.RI 3x timeout ,
at least 20 s, deliberately longer than one exchange: it is there to stop a
wedged invocation, and must not itself cut ipmitool's own retries short.
.TP
.IR message_timeout ", " tool_retries
ipmitool's own
.BR \-N " and " \-R .
Both default to null, meaning the flags are not passed and ipmitool's defaults
(four retries) stand \(em which is what the known-working upstream invocation
relies on. Forcing a single attempt turns a BMC that needs a retry into an
"Unable to establish IPMI v2 / RMCP+ session" failure.
.TP
.I extra_args
Extra ipmitool arguments, appended verbatim, e.g.
.B ['-e', '^']
for the SOL escape character.
.TP
.I power_on_delay
Seconds to wait after
.B chassis power on
before the first SSH attempt.
.TP
.I stop_on_auth_failure
Stop issuing IPMI commands once a BMC has answered and rejected the
credentials \(em an RAKP failure, or "unauthorized name". One credential set
serves every BMC, so the next one rejects it too, and each attempt is
.I retries
invocations here with four ipmitool retries inside each. Defaults to
.BR true .
Set it false only to gather evidence from several BMCs deliberately; the run
then sends the same rejected username to every controller in the cluster.
A BMC that simply does not answer is not this \(em a dark chassis reports
"Unable to establish IPMI v2 / RMCP+ session" as well, and after an outage
that is the expected case.
.SS kerberos
.TP
.IR principal ", " root_principal
Principals to use. Null means "use whatever is in the ambient credential
cache". When set, each gets its own private cache, destroyed when the run ends.
.TP
.I min_lifetime
Remaining ticket lifetime, in seconds, below which a ticket is renewed up front
rather than left to expire mid-phase.
.TP
.I prompt
Prompt for a password when a ticket is missing. Set false for unattended runs;
the run then fails with instructions instead.
.TP
.I verify_users
Accounts whose ability to log in is itself a check
.RI ( login.users ).
.TP
.I use_service_keytabs
Fall back to the Mu2e service identities when the operator's own ticket is
refused by a host. Default
.BR true .
The operator's own principal is always tried first regardless; these are only
ever fallbacks, and the run returns to the personal ticket after each node.
.TP
.I root_fallback
Apply the same fallback to root sessions. Default
.BR true .
The login stays
.B root
and only the ticket changes, which a node's
.I root/.k5login
can authorise.
Tickets come from the
.B mu2edaq-kerberos
package, which owns the keytab-in-Vault layout at
.IR <base_path>/kerberos/<identity> .
.TP
.I service_identities
Identities to try first, in order. Default
.BR "[mu2edaq, mu2eshift]" ,
which between them cover most of the cluster, so the common case is one extra
attempt rather than the whole list.
.TP
.I discover_identities
After the preferred ones, try every other identity
.B mu2edaq-kerberos
knows about. Default
.BR true ,
so an identity added to Vault becomes usable with no change here.
.TP
.IR get_kerberos_ticket_command ", " vault_client_command
Paths to the
.B mu2edaq-kerberos
commands. Left null they are found on
.BR PATH ,
then in a sibling
.I ../mu2edaq-kerberos
checkout's venv.
.TP
.I vault_client_args
Extra arguments passed through to
.BR vault-client ,
for example
.BR "['--org', 'mu2e']" .
.SS vault
.TP
.IR addr ", " kv_mount ", " base_path
Vault address, KV v2 mount and the base path under it. Defaults address
.IR https://ssivault.fnal.gov:8200 ,
mount
.IR td ,
base path
.IR scd/experiments/mu2e .
.TP
.I ipmi_path
Secret holding the BMC credentials, relative to
.IR base_path .
Default
.IR ipmi/config .
.I ipmi
is a folder in the KV tree, not the secret; pointing this at the folder returns
no fields at all, which looks the same as an empty secret.
.BR mu2e-vault-ipmi (1)
lists the tree when a path holds no secret, so the two are distinguishable.
.TP
.IR ipmi_user_field ", " ipmi_password_field
Field names inside that secret. Default
.IR username " and " password ,
confirmed against the live secret. Kept configurable because the secret is
maintained outside this repository and could be re-keyed; the client also
accepts the obvious synonyms and logs which it used. Check with
.BR mu2e-vault-ipmi (1).
.TP
.IR allow_file_fallback ", " fallback_password_file ", " fallback_user
Fall back to a local password file when Vault is unreachable. A site-wide power
event is exactly when the Vault server may also be down, and that must not be
the reason the DAQ cannot be powered back on. The fallback is recorded in the
run.
.TP
.I auto_login
Run
.B vault login \-method=ldap
when no usable token is cached. A token is looked for first in
.BR VAULT_TOKEN ,
then in
.IR ~/.vault-token .
The CLI is shelled out to rather than reimplemented: it is what the site
documents and it already handles the MFA prompt. Set this false for an
unattended run, which then fails with the
.B vault login
line to run by hand.
.TP
.I timeout
Seconds allowed for one HTTPS request to Vault. Default
.BR 30 .
This does
.I not
bound the interactive
.BR "vault login" ,
which is allowed five minutes because a human has to answer an MFA prompt
inside it.
.SS database
.TP
.I url
Full SQLAlchemy URL. Set a
.I postgresql+psycopg2://
URL to use Postgres.
.TP
.I path
SQLite path used when
.I url
is null.
.SS report
.TP
.IR output_dir ", " title
Where the static site is written, and its heading.
.TP
.I keep_runs
How many archived runs to keep under
.IR output_dir/runs/ .
.TP
.I publish
.IR enabled ", " method
.RB ( rsync ", " scp ", " copy ", " none ),
.IR target ", " options .
.I options
is passed verbatim to
.BR rsync (1)
or
.BR scp (1)
and ignored by
.BR copy ,
which is a local
.BR cp (1)-equivalent
for a target that is a mounted web area.
.IP
Publication failure is never fatal: the report on disk is the artefact, and a
site-wide power event is a plausible reason for the web host to be unreachable.
An unknown
.I method
is reported the same way rather than guessing.
.IP
A simulated run
.RB ( \-\-simulate )
never publishes, whatever this section says, and gives that as its reason. The
.B copy
method writes with the local filesystem directly rather than through the
scripted transport, so a rehearsal would otherwise overwrite the live web area
with rehearsal output.
.SS ecl
.TP
.I enabled
Post the phase-4 report to the electronic logbook.
.TP
.IR url ", " category
ECL instance and category path.
.TP
.I credential_path
Vault path, relative to
.IR vault.base_path ,
holding the ECL API user and key.
.TP
.I attach_html
Attach the generated pages to the entry.
.SS logging
.TP
.IR level ", " file
Console level and the rotating log file.
.TP
.I capture_command_output
Intended switch for keeping remote command output in the run store.
.B Not currently consulted:
output is captured either way. Turn the volume down with
.I max_capture_bytes
instead.
.TP
.I max_capture_bytes
How much of one command's output is kept, in bytes. Default
.BR 65536 .
Output beyond it is dropped at the transport, before it reaches the run store.
This is the evidence trail the report and the logbook entry are built from, so
lowering it costs detail in the report, not just disk.
.SH EXAMPLES
Override one setting for a single run, without editing anything:
.RS
.EX
MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_SSH_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=30 mu2e-power-state
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use Postgres instead of SQLite:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-recovery \-\-database\-url \e
    postgresql+psycopg2://mu2e@dbhost/power_recovery
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2edaq-topology.yaml (5),
.BR mu2edaq-power-sequence.yaml (5),
.BR mu2edaq-checks.yaml (5)
.SH AUTHOR
A. Norman <anorman@fnal.gov>, Mu2e DAQ Operations.
